    "turn_text": "right. You're absolutely right. Look, I was thinking about this when you were talking about Afghanistan and it kind of slipped my mind and it came back into my mind while you were talking here. The fact that I've been, I spent a lot of time in Afghanistan, many, many years covering the war there. I would go into villages. I wasn't just with our US military forces, which were amazing, amazing men and women in the region that Some of them gave their full life. Others gave their eyes like my husband or an arm or a leg. They were fighting the fight there, but I would go into the villages and I would see mothers, mothers in those villages that would do anything and everything to save their child in the poorest place on earth with nothing you would think with no hope because they were being abused by Taliban leaders, not having enough rights, whatever you want to say. The women there, but there were women that loved their children so much that they would sacrifice their own life for their child, even in poverty. So when I hear these arguments coming from here, It blows my mind because I've been to countries all over the world where mothers have walked across deserts to get their kids food. It's almost as if we're living in upside down land. When you just talked about our national security and our foreign policy, and you see what's happening in China, you see what's happening in North Korea, you see what's happening in Japan, in Taiwan, you know, the world is literally upside down. And we have no leadership in this country. Nobody is facing the reality that in the next few years things could be far worse for us here because what is happening over there. And it almost feels that the administration is moving it in that direction at full speed.",
